                <h2>Pre-Setup: Define Your Tiering Objectives</h2>
<p>Before implementing account tiering, align your team on the core purpose: it's not about controlling accounts—it's about <strong>concentrating resources, improving efficiency, and distributing risk</strong>. Complete these preparation steps first:</p>
<ul>
<li>Inventory all current advertising accounts (platform, region, budget scale)</li>
<li>Assign tier ownership (who manages which tier of accounts daily)</li>
<li>Prepare tiering spreadsheets or team collaboration tools for tracking</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>SOP Step 1: Establish Tiering Criteria</h2>
<p>Based on industry best practices, classify accounts using these dimensions:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Tier</th>
<th>Naming Example</th>
<th>Key Characteristics</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>S-Tier (Core)</strong></td>
<td>Account-Prime-01</td>
<td>Daily budget &gt; $5,000, consistent ROI, lifetime spend &gt; $100K</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>A-Tier (Main)</strong></td>
<td>Account-Main-01</td>
<td>Daily budget $1,000-$5,000, stable conversion data</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>B-Tier (Testing)</strong></td>
<td>Account-Test-01</td>
<td>Daily budget &lt; $1,000, new creatives or market testing</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>C-Tier (Backup)</strong></td>
<td>Account-Backup-01</td>
<td>Currently inactive or under observation</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Key Point</strong>: Re-evaluate tier classifications quarterly and adjust based on performance data.</p>
<hr />
<h2>SOP Step 2: Classify and Tag Accounts</h2>
<ol>
<li>Apply uniform naming labels to each account (e.g., <code>Tier-S</code>, <code>Region-SEA</code>)</li>
<li>Record in your ad management dashboard or Excel: Account ID, tier level, platform, primary campaign objective</li>
<li>Set differentiated permissions between tiers (e.g., S/A-tier accounts require dual authorization for budget changes)</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>SOP Step 3: Configure Tiered Budget and Bidding Strategies</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Tier</th>
<th>Budget Adjustment Authority</th>
<th>Bidding Strategy</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>S-Tier</td>
<td>Requires supervisor approval, ≤30% per change</td>
<td>Target ROAS priority</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>A-Tier</td>
<td>Operator autonomy, ≤50% per change</td>
<td>Balance cost and volume</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>B-Tier</td>
<td>Flexible permissions, rapid iteration</td>
<td>Learning-phase volume focus</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>C-Tier</td>
<td>Monitoring only, no active delivery</td>
<td>Awaiting activation</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<hr />
<h2>SOP Step 4: Daily Monitoring Checklist</h2>
<p><strong>S/A-Tier Accounts</strong>:<br />
- Check daily spend vs. KPI deviation (alert if &gt;15% deviation)<br />
- Review high-cost ad groups, evaluate pause eligibility<br />
- Confirm sufficient balance, trigger refill alert (balance &lt; 3 days' spend)</p>
<p><strong>B-Tier Accounts</strong>:<br />
- Review test creative CTR/CVR data<br />
- Flag high performers, evaluate migration to A-Tier<br />
- Pause ad groups with zero conversions for 3+ consecutive days</p>
<p><strong>C-Tier Accounts</strong>:<br />
- Verify account status (no bans or review flags)<br />
- Log observation period metrics for upgrade/shutdown decisions</p>
<hr />
<h2>SOP Step 5: Risk Control Triggers</h2>
<p>Activate emergency response when:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Single account daily spend anomaly</strong> (&gt;200% of daily budget) → Pause immediately, investigate</li>
<li><strong>Platform risk trigger</strong> (review/ban warning) → Migrate budget to backup accounts</li>
<li><strong>Multi-account performance decline</strong> (&gt;20% conversion drop) → Pause new ad creation, audit creative compliance first</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>SOP Step 6: Tiering Reviews and Iteration (Weekly/Monthly)</h2>
<p><strong>Weekly</strong>: Verify budget consumption ratios across tiers, confirm resource allocation合理性.</p>
<p><strong>Monthly</strong>: Review upgrade/downgrade cases, update tiering documentation.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Quick FAQ</h2>
<p><strong>Q1: Where should new accounts be placed initially?</strong><br />
Place new accounts in the B-Tier testing pool. Reclassify after 2-4 weeks based on actual performance data.</p>
<p><strong>Q2: Can S-Tier accounts be downgraded?</strong><br />
Yes. If ROI falls below baseline by 30% for 2+ consecutive weeks, initiate downgrade process and reallocate resources.</p>
<p><strong>Q3: How to unify tiering across multiple platforms?</strong><br />
Use the "parent + child" account model. Accounts under the same entity share tier labels for streamlined financial reconciliation.</p>
<hr />
<p>By following these 6 steps, multi-account advertising teams can establish a clear account management framework that ensures resource allocation is targeted and risk control is systematic.</p>
